# Code Analysis: Counting Numbers with Odd Set Bits

## Main Goal

This code counts how many integers in the range **[1, n]** have an **odd number of set bits** (odd popcount) in their binary representation. It computes this efficiently in **O(1)** time (without iterating through all numbers) by exploiting a mathematical pattern.

## How It Works

**Key Insight:** Among any two consecutive integers, exactly one has an odd number of set bits and the other has an even number of set bits (since incrementing a number flips the trailing 1s to 0s and one 0 to 1, changing the parity of set bits in most cases, and this pairing pattern holds for consecutive pairs).

### Case 1: `n` is odd
```java
if (n % 2 != 0) {
    return (n + 1) / 2;
}
```
When `n` is odd, the numbers from 1 to n form exactly `(n+1)/2` complete odd-even pairs, and exactly half of the total count corresponds to odd-set-bit numbers. So the answer is simply `(n+1)/2`.

### Case 2: `n` is even
```java
long count = Long.bitCount(n);
long ans = n / 2;
if (count % 2 != 0) {
    ans++;
}
return ans;
```
